Subject: [PATCH 14/14] Give the library numbered favorites a controller can
 store and recall

[AI-generated text: this description was written autonomously by an AI
agent (Claude). Review it before relying on it or submitting it.]

A controller can walk the sidebar one row at a time, but it cannot go
straight to a given crate, playlist or folder. On an appliance where
visiting DJs plug in drives with Rekordbox or Serato libraries, the
playlist a DJ wants is several levels down a tree that has to be
scrolled with a knob, every time.

LibraryFavorites adds six numbered favorites as controls:

  [Library],favorite_N_store     remember the selected sidebar item
  [Library],favorite_N_activate  select and open it again
  [Library],favorite_N_status    read-only: 0 empty, 1 stored
  [Library],favorite_recalls     read-only: counts successful recalls

A favorite is the feature's icon name plus a key for the item: its data
where it has any (a crate or playlist id, a folder path, the path of a
Rekordbox or Serato playlist, which starts with the drive's mount path),
and its label path. The data survives renames and the counts some labels
carry; the label path tells apart items that share their data (History's
year folders) and is the only key for items with none (the folder levels
of nested Serato crates). Rekordbox and Serato keep a loaded/not-loaded
flag next to the path, which changes when the item is first opened, so a
list is keyed by its first element only.

Recall searches the part of the feature's tree that is already loaded,
selects the item, expands everything above it and opens it through the
sidebar's own click path, so every feature behaves as it does when the
item is clicked. An item that is not there - a drive that has been
unplugged, or not opened since it was plugged in - is not searched for
further and nothing happens; the recall counter does not move either, so
a mapping can switch pages only when there is something to show.
Favorites are kept in the config under [LibraryFavorites].

LibraryControl owns the favorites and now takes the config. SidebarModel
exposes its feature list, the feature an index belongs to, and the
sidebar index of a tree item. WLibrarySidebar's selectedIndex() is
public, and activateIndex() selects, expands, scrolls and clicks.

libraryfavorites_test covers the keys (data, list data, label path),
finding deep items, a label that changed, shared data told apart by
label, items without data, a gone item, a feature root, and the text
round trip.

[End of AI-generated text.]

